// Practice quiz: Reading: session cookies vs JWTs, trade-offs
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/api-security/session-cookies-vs-jwts-trade-offs

::o1 state::Where is login state stored with server-side sessions? {
=On the server, identified by an id in a cookie#Correct. The cookie is just a reference.
~Entirely in the cookie#Not quite. Only an id is there.
~In the JWT#Not quite. That is the token approach.
~In the URL#Not quite. That is unsafe, and it is not how sessions work here.
}

::o1 revocation::Why is revoking a plain JWT before expiry hard? {
=A valid signature stays valid with no server state to check#Correct. Revocation needs an extra check or short lifetimes.
~JWTs cannot expire#Not quite. They can.
~JWTs are encrypted#Not quite. They are usually signed, not encrypted, and that is unrelated.
~Browsers block JWTs#Not quite. They do not.
}

::o2 cookies::Why does CSRF concern cookie-based logins? {
=Browsers send cookies automatically with matching requests#Correct. Another site can cause those requests.
~Cookies are always unencrypted#Not quite. That is not what causes CSRF.
~Cookies are large#Not quite. Size is not the cause.
~Servers cannot read cookies#Not quite. They can.
}

::o2 bearer::Why is a bearer token in a header not vulnerable to the same CSRF path? {
=The browser does not attach it automatically#Correct. Your code adds it.
~Headers are encrypted separately#Not quite. They are not.
~Tokens are shorter#Not quite. Length is not why.
~HTTP forbids CSRF#Not quite. It does not.
}

::o3 choosing::A first-party browser app with one backend needs immediate logout. What fits well? {
=A server-side session in an HttpOnly cookie#Correct. Revocation is simple, and scripts cannot read the cookie.
~A 30-day JWT in localStorage#Not quite. That is hard to revoke and readable by scripts.
~A password on each request#Not quite. That spreads the password.
~No authentication#Not quite. That is not an option here.
}

